begin model

#@title: mTOR network activation by amino acids and insulin (Dalle Pezze et al., 2016)

#@description: |
#  The mTOR signalling network of C2C12 myoblasts, resolved as phosphorylation
#  states of eleven nodes: the insulin receptor (IR), IRS1, two PI3K activities,
#  Akt, mTORC1, mTORC2, AMPK, the TSC1-TSC2 complex, p70-S6K and PRAS40. The
#  model is the paper's best-fit "Model 3" (AIC ~680): amino acids enter the
#  network at FOUR points — mTORC1-S2448, IRS1, AMPK-T172 and mTORC2-S2481 —
#  and p70-S6K carries an extended module in which PI3K/PDK1 phosphorylates
#  T229 as well as mTORC1 phosphorylating T389.
#
#  How it works: every reaction is mass action. Interconversion between
#  phosphostates is either first order (dephosphorylation, IR recycling,
#  IRS1-pS636 turnover) or pseudo-first order in a modifier that is itself a
#  network node, written here as a rate-law function of an observable. Akt,
#  p70-S6K and PRAS40 are two-site molecules, and each of their four transitions
#  carries its OWN rate constant: the published fit does not constrain the
#  "first" and "second" modifications to be equal, and five of the twelve pairs
#  differ by more than an order of magnitude (see #@note below). Feedback is
#  negative and two-armed: doubly-phosphorylated p70-S6K drives IRS1 to the
#  inactive pS636 state from BOTH the naive and the tyrosine-phosphorylated
#  pool, and AMPK-pT172 inhibits mTORC1 through TSC1-TSC2-pS1387.
#
#  This is the amino-acid PLUS INSULIN condition, and it differs from the
#  primary file by one seed amount: Ins_0 = 1 rather than 0. That single change
#  switches on the whole insulin arm, which is inert in the primary file — the
#  receptor cycle, the IR-driven activation of IRS1 and of the PI3K variant,
#  and with them a twelfth readout, IR-pY1146, that has nothing to report when
#  insulin is absent. It is the fuller network, not a perturbation of the
#  primary one.
#
#  What it does: insulin makes the response biphasic where amino acids alone
#  make it monotone. IR-pY1146 spikes within about a minute and then decays as
#  receptor accumulates in the refractory state, and Akt-pT308 follows it with
#  a sharp peak near 66 relative units that relaxes to a plateau near 39 — the
#  signature of the negative feedback from p70-S6K onto IRS1. Readouts are an
#  order of magnitude larger than in the aa-only condition and the axes of
#  Fig. 2b are scaled accordingly.

#@keyword: |
#  signalling, mTOR, AMPK, amino acid sensing, phosphorylation cascade,
#  negative feedback, mass action, monostable, dimensionless,
#  Mus musculus

#@reference: |
#  Dalle Pezze P, Ruf S, Sonntag AG, Langelaar-Makkinje M, Hall P, Heberle AM,
#  Razquin Navas P, van Eunen K, Tolle RC, Schwarz JJ, Wiese H, Warscheid B,
#  Deitersen J, Stork B, Fassler E, Schauble S, Hahn U, Horvatovich P,
#  Shanley DP, Thedieck K (2016). A systems study reveals concurrent activation
#  of AMPK and mTOR by amino acids. Nature Communications 7:13254.
#  doi:10.1038/ncomms13254
#  Model source: BioModels BIOMD0000000640 (curated, CC0), the deposit of the
#  paper's Supplementary Software 3.

#@note: |
#  SIMULATION INTENT. This model is dimensionless and ODE-only. Amounts are
#  arbitrary relative units, not molecule counts and not concentrations: the
#  authors fixed each unphosphorylated pool "to a value that avoided
#  phosphorylation level saturation", and the readouts are immunoblot relative
#  intensities that "cannot be compared among readouts" (Fig. 2 caption in
#  Dalle Pezze et al., 2016). No volume appears anywhere in the paper and the
#  deposited SBML declares no units, so per skills/bngl/skill.md §1.1 there is
#  no NA, no V_ref, no compartments block and no SSA or NFsim protocol —
#  stochastic simulation of a 20-unit PRAS40 pool would be meaningless, not
#  merely non-preferred.

#@note: |
#  CONTEXT-SPECIFIC RATES, NOT A SYMMETRY. Akt, p70-S6K and PRAS40 each have
#  two modifiable sites, so BNGL expresses their topology in four rules per
#  site pair. The rate constants are NOT shared between contexts. The paper
#  says it constrained "the parameter values of the corresponding first and
#  second reactions" for Akt and PRAS40, but the deposited best-fit values are
#  not equal even there, and p70-S6K was never covered by the constraint:
#  Akt-S473 phosphorylation is 1.32e-5 on unmodified Akt against 0.159 on
#  Akt-pT308 (1.2e4-fold), p70-S6K-pT229 dephosphorylation 1.0e-6 against 0.159
#  (1.6e5-fold), p70-S6K-T389 phosphorylation 0.0026 against 0.111 (42-fold),
#  p70-S6K-T229 phosphorylation 0.0134 against 1.0e-6, and PRAS40-S183
#  phosphorylation 0.159 against 0.068. Collapsing each pair onto one constant
#  changes ten of the twelve observables — Akt-pS473 falls by 99.8% of its peak
#  and p70-S6K-pT229 rises ninefold — so the per-context rates are load-bearing
#  and are transcribed one by one below.

#@note: |
#  NAMING. Node names follow the paper rather than MGI gene symbols. Six of the
#  eleven are not single gene products (mTORC1, mTORC2 and TSC1-TSC2 are
#  complexes; PI3K_PDK1 and PI3K_var are composite activities the authors
#  introduced to separate two wortmannin-sensitive inputs), so the §4.2 symbol
#  convention does not apply cleanly and renaming the remainder would make the
#  file unreadable against the paper. Same deviation, same reason, as
#  models/insulin_signalling_and_oxidative_stress_smith2013.

#@note: |
#  WORTMANNIN IS NOT A SPECIES. The deposited model contains no wortmannin and
#  no inhibitor term; the string does not occur in BIOMD0000000640. Fig. 2a
#  draws wortmannin because it is an SBGN diagram of the biology. Inhibition
#  was simulated by reducing initial amounts — "for 10 levels of protein from
#  10 to 100% (control) for mTORC1, mTORC2, PI3K and AMPK ... this was achieved
#  by modifying initial amounts" (Methods) — which is what the level parameters
#  do. The wortmannin condition of Fig. 2d is PI3K_level = 0.1, the paper's
#  own "residual activity 10%" (Fig. 1d caption).

begin parameters
  # --- Initial pool sizes (arbitrary relative units; BIOMD0000000640 initial
  # amounts). Every phosphorylated state starts at zero: "the initial amount
  # for the phosphorylated states was fixed to 0" (Methods).
  IR_0          50    # dimensionless
  IRS_0        150    # dimensionless
  PI3K_var_0    50    # dimensionless
  PI3K_PDK1_0   50    # dimensionless
  Akt_0        300    # dimensionless
  mTORC2_0     100    # dimensionless
  AMPK_0        50    # dimensionless
  TSC_0         50    # dimensionless
  mTORC1_0     100    # dimensionless
  S6K_0        300    # dimensionless
  PRAS40_0      20    # dimensionless

  # --- Network inputs. Both are held constant over the simulated window; the
  # deposited model drives them with piecewise rules that are flat for t >= 0.
  # This file is the amino-acid PLUS INSULIN condition of Fig. 2b.
  Ins_0          1    # dimensionless
  AA_0           1    # dimensionless

  # --- Node activity levels, as fractions of control. The paper's four
  # perturbation experiments (scanned in the primary file) reduce the
  # corresponding initial amounts (Methods); PI3K perturbation moves both PI3K
  # activities together, which is what reproduces the wortmannin condition.
  PI3K_level     1.0  # dimensionless
  mTORC1_level   1.0  # dimensionless
  mTORC2_level   1.0  # dimensionless
  AMPK_level     1.0  # dimensionless

  # --- Amino-acid inputs (the four that define this model; Table 1)
  IRS_phos_by_Amino_Acids                      0.0331672   # /min
  AMPK_T172_phos_by_Amino_Acids                17.6284     # /min
  mTORC2_S2481_phos_by_Amino_Acids             0.0268658   # /min
  mTORC1_S2448_activation_by_Amino_Acids       0.0156992   # /min

  # --- Insulin receptor: activation, inactivation to a refractory state, and
  # recycling. The refractory state is what makes IR signalling transient.
  IR_beta_phos_by_Insulin                      0.0203796   # /min
  IR_beta_pY1146_dephos                        0.493514    # /min
  IR_beta_ready                                323.611     # /min

  # --- IRS1, including the two arms of the p70-S6K negative feedback
  IRS_phos_by_IR_beta_pY1146                   2.11894     # /min
  IRS_p_phos_by_p70_S6K_pT229_pT389            0.338859859949792   # /min
  IRS_phos_by_p70_S6K_pT229_pT389              0.0863775267376444  # /min
  IRS_pS636_turnover                           25          # /min

  # --- PI3K/PDK1 and the PI3K variant that feeds mTORC2
  PI3K_PDK1_phos_by_IRS_p                      0.000187226757782201  # /min
  PI3K_p_PDK1_dephos                           0.18913343080532      # /min
  PI3K_variant_phos_by_IR_beta_pY1146          0.000549027801822575  # /min
  PI3K_variant_p_dephos                        0.108074886441184     # /min

  # --- AMPK
  AMPK_T172_phos                               0.490602    # /min
  AMPK_pT172_dephos                            165.704     # /min

  # --- Akt. `_first` acts on Akt unmodified at the other site, `_second` on Akt
  # already modified there; the two are independent fitted constants.
  Akt_T308_phos_by_PI3K_p_PDK1_first           7.47437     # /min
  Akt_T308_phos_by_PI3K_p_PDK1_second          7.47345     # /min
  Akt_S473_phos_by_mTORC2_pS2481_first         1.31992e-5  # /min
  Akt_S473_phos_by_mTORC2_pS2481_second        0.159093    # /min
  Akt_pT308_dephos_first                       88.9654     # /min
  Akt_pT308_dephos_second                      88.9639     # /min
  Akt_pS473_dephos_first                       0.376999    # /min
  Akt_pS473_dephos_second                      0.380005    # /min

  # --- mTORC2
  mTORC2_S2481_phos_by_PI3K_variant_p          0.120736    # /min
  mTORC2_pS2481_dephos                         1.42511     # /min

  # --- TSC1-TSC2. S1387 is the AMPK site, T1462 the Akt site; the doubly
  # phosphorylated complex does not exist in the published model.
  TSC1_TSC2_S1387_phos_by_AMPK_pT172           0.00175772  # /min
  TSC1_TSC2_T1462_phos_by_Akt_pT308            1.52417     # /min
  TSC1_TSC2_pS1387_dephos                      0.25319     # /min
  TSC1_TSC2_pT1462_dephos                      147.239     # /min

  # --- mTORC1. Only the S1387-phosphorylated and naive TSC species inhibit it;
  # TSC-pT1462 (the Akt-inhibited form) does not.
  mTORC1_pS2448_dephos_by_TSC1_TSC2            0.00869774  # /min

  # --- p70-S6K, extended module. T389 is the mTORC1 site, T229 the PI3K/PDK1
  # site added in this model.
  p70_S6K_T389_phos_by_mTORC1_pS2448_first     0.00261303413778722  # /min
  p70_S6K_T389_phos_by_mTORC1_pS2448_second    0.110720890919343    # /min
  p70_S6K_T229_phos_by_PI3K_p_PDK1_first       0.0133520172873009   # /min
  p70_S6K_T229_phos_by_PI3K_p_PDK1_second      1.00000002814509e-6  # /min
  p70_S6K_pT389_dephos_first                   1.10036057608758     # /min
  p70_S6K_pT389_dephos_second                  1.10215267954479     # /min
  p70_S6K_pT229_dephos_first                   1.00000012897033e-6  # /min
  p70_S6K_pT229_dephos_second                  0.159201353240651    # /min

  # --- PRAS40. S183 is the mTORC1 site, T246 the Akt site.
  PRAS40_S183_phos_by_mTORC1_pS2448_first      0.15881     # /min
  PRAS40_S183_phos_by_mTORC1_pS2448_second     0.0683009   # /min
  PRAS40_T246_phos_by_Akt_pT308_first          0.279344    # /min
  PRAS40_T246_phos_by_Akt_pT308_second         0.279401    # /min
  PRAS40_pS183_dephos_first                    1.8706      # /min
  PRAS40_pS183_dephos_second                   1.88453     # /min
  PRAS40_pT246_dephos_first                    11.8759     # /min
  PRAS40_pT246_dephos_second                   11.876      # /min
end parameters

begin molecule types
  # Insulin receptor beta chain: naive, Y1146-phosphorylated, refractory
  IR(state~u~p~r)
  # IRS1: naive, tyrosine-phosphorylated (active), S636-phosphorylated (feedback-inactivated)
  IRS(state~u~p~pS636)
  # PI3K activity upstream of mTORC2, driven by IR and not by IRS1
  PI3K_var(s~u~p)
  # The PI3K/PDK1 activity that phosphorylates Akt-T308 and p70-S6K-T229
  PI3K_PDK1(s~u~p)
  Akt(t308~u~p,s473~u~p)
  mTORC2(s2481~u~p)
  AMPK(t172~u~p)
  # TSC1-TSC2 complex; three states only, the doubly modified form is not in the model
  TSC(state~u~pS1387~pT1462)
  mTORC1(s2448~u~p)
  S6K(t229~u~p,t389~u~p)
  PRAS40(s183~u~p,t246~u~p)
  # Fixed network inputs, held constant by the `$` prefix in seed species
  Ins()
  AA()
end molecule types

begin seed species
  # Every pool starts fully unphosphorylated. The four `*_level` factors are 1
  # in this file and are what the perturbation scans move.
  IR(state~u)                                IR_0                       # dimensionless
  IRS(state~u)                               IRS_0                      # dimensionless
  PI3K_var(s~u)                              PI3K_level*PI3K_var_0      # dimensionless
  PI3K_PDK1(s~u)                             PI3K_level*PI3K_PDK1_0     # dimensionless
  Akt(t308~u,s473~u)                         Akt_0                      # dimensionless
  mTORC2(s2481~u)                            mTORC2_level*mTORC2_0      # dimensionless
  AMPK(t172~u)                               AMPK_level*AMPK_0          # dimensionless
  TSC(state~u)                               TSC_0                      # dimensionless
  mTORC1(s2448~u)                            mTORC1_level*mTORC1_0      # dimensionless
  S6K(t229~u,t389~u)                         S6K_0                      # dimensionless
  PRAS40(s183~u,t246~u)                      PRAS40_0                   # dimensionless
  $Ins()                                     Ins_0                      # dimensionless
  $AA()                                      AA_0                       # dimensionless
end seed species

begin observables
  # Network inputs, plotted as the first two panels of Fig. 2b-d
  Molecules Obs_Insulin           Ins()
  Molecules Obs_AminoAcids        AA()

  # The twelve model readouts of Fig. 2. For the two-site nodes the paper's
  # observable is the total phosphorylation of one site — "Akt-pS473-obs was
  # associated to the sum of the species Akt-pS473 and Akt-pT308-pS473"
  # (Methods) — which is exactly what a one-site BNGL pattern counts.
  Molecules Obs_IR_pY1146         IR(state~p)
  Molecules Obs_IRS_pS636         IRS(state~pS636)
  Molecules Obs_AMPK_pT172        AMPK(t172~p)
  Molecules Obs_Akt_pT308         Akt(t308~p)
  Molecules Obs_Akt_pS473         Akt(s473~p)
  Molecules Obs_TSC_pS1387        TSC(state~pS1387)
  Molecules Obs_mTOR_pS2448       mTORC1(s2448~p)
  Molecules Obs_mTOR_pS2481       mTORC2(s2481~p)
  Molecules Obs_S6K_pT229         S6K(t229~p)
  Molecules Obs_S6K_pT389         S6K(t389~p)
  Molecules Obs_PRAS40_pT246      PRAS40(t246~p)
  Molecules Obs_PRAS40_pS183      PRAS40(s183~p)

  # Internal drivers. These are not paper readouts; they carry rate laws.
  # IRS-p is the active adaptor, and doubly phosphorylated p70-S6K is the
  # negative-feedback enzyme.
  Molecules Obs_IRS_p             IRS(state~p)
  Molecules Obs_PI3K_var_p        PI3K_var(s~p)
  Molecules Obs_PI3K_PDK1_p       PI3K_PDK1(s~p)
  Molecules Obs_S6K_pT229_pT389   S6K(t229~p,t389~p)
  Molecules Obs_TSC_u             TSC(state~u)
end observables

begin functions
  # Every function below is the modifier-dependent part of a pseudo-first-order
  # rate law. BioNetGen supplies the substrate factor itself (§1.2.5), so these
  # carry only the rate constant and its enzyme.

  # Insulin receptor activation
  f_IR_phos()             = IR_beta_phos_by_Insulin*Obs_Insulin

  # IRS1 activation by the receptor and, independently, by amino acids
  f_IRS_phos_by_IR()      = IRS_phos_by_IR_beta_pY1146*Obs_IR_pY1146
  f_IRS_phos_by_AA()      = IRS_phos_by_Amino_Acids*Obs_AminoAcids

  # p70-S6K negative feedback onto IRS1, acting on both the active and the
  # naive pool
  f_IRSp_to_pS636()       = IRS_p_phos_by_p70_S6K_pT229_pT389*Obs_S6K_pT229_pT389
  f_IRSu_to_pS636()       = IRS_phos_by_p70_S6K_pT229_pT389*Obs_S6K_pT229_pT389

  # The two PI3K activities
  f_PI3K_var_phos()       = PI3K_variant_phos_by_IR_beta_pY1146*Obs_IR_pY1146
  f_PI3K_PDK1_phos()      = PI3K_PDK1_phos_by_IRS_p*Obs_IRS_p

  # Akt, per context
  f_Akt_T308_first()      = Akt_T308_phos_by_PI3K_p_PDK1_first*Obs_PI3K_PDK1_p
  f_Akt_T308_second()     = Akt_T308_phos_by_PI3K_p_PDK1_second*Obs_PI3K_PDK1_p
  f_Akt_S473_first()      = Akt_S473_phos_by_mTORC2_pS2481_first*Obs_mTOR_pS2481
  f_Akt_S473_second()     = Akt_S473_phos_by_mTORC2_pS2481_second*Obs_mTOR_pS2481

  # mTORC2, from the PI3K variant and from amino acids
  f_mTORC2_by_PI3K_var()  = mTORC2_S2481_phos_by_PI3K_variant_p*Obs_PI3K_var_p
  f_mTORC2_by_AA()        = mTORC2_S2481_phos_by_Amino_Acids*Obs_AminoAcids

  # AMPK, from active IRS1 and from amino acids
  f_AMPK_phos_by_IRSp()   = AMPK_T172_phos*Obs_IRS_p
  f_AMPK_phos_by_AA()     = AMPK_T172_phos_by_Amino_Acids*Obs_AminoAcids

  # TSC1-TSC2
  f_TSC_S1387()           = TSC1_TSC2_S1387_phos_by_AMPK_pT172*Obs_AMPK_pT172
  f_TSC_T1462()           = TSC1_TSC2_T1462_phos_by_Akt_pT308*Obs_Akt_pT308

  # mTORC1. Inhibition is by naive plus S1387-phosphorylated TSC only.
  f_mTORC1_by_AA()        = mTORC1_S2448_activation_by_Amino_Acids*Obs_AminoAcids
  f_mTORC1_dephos()       = mTORC1_pS2448_dephos_by_TSC1_TSC2*(Obs_TSC_u + Obs_TSC_pS1387)

  # p70-S6K, per context
  f_S6K_T389_first()      = p70_S6K_T389_phos_by_mTORC1_pS2448_first*Obs_mTOR_pS2448
  f_S6K_T389_second()     = p70_S6K_T389_phos_by_mTORC1_pS2448_second*Obs_mTOR_pS2448
  f_S6K_T229_first()      = p70_S6K_T229_phos_by_PI3K_p_PDK1_first*Obs_PI3K_PDK1_p
  f_S6K_T229_second()     = p70_S6K_T229_phos_by_PI3K_p_PDK1_second*Obs_PI3K_PDK1_p

  # PRAS40, per context
  f_PRAS40_S183_first()   = PRAS40_S183_phos_by_mTORC1_pS2448_first*Obs_mTOR_pS2448
  f_PRAS40_S183_second()  = PRAS40_S183_phos_by_mTORC1_pS2448_second*Obs_mTOR_pS2448
  f_PRAS40_T246_first()   = PRAS40_T246_phos_by_Akt_pT308_first*Obs_Akt_pT308
  f_PRAS40_T246_second()  = PRAS40_T246_phos_by_Akt_pT308_second*Obs_Akt_pT308
end functions

begin reaction rules
  # --- Insulin receptor: a three-state cycle. Activation is insulin driven;
  # the pY1146 form decays to a refractory state rather than straight back to
  # naive, which is how the model makes the insulin response transient.
  R_phos_IR_Y1146:        IR(state~u) -> IR(state~p)                    f_IR_phos()
  R_inact_IR_refractory:  IR(state~p) -> IR(state~r)                    IR_beta_pY1146_dephos
  R_recycle_IR:           IR(state~r) -> IR(state~u)                    IR_beta_ready

  # --- IRS1. Two activating inputs and two feedback inputs converge on the
  # same three states; the pS636 form turns over back to naive.
  R_phos_IRS_by_IR:       IRS(state~u) -> IRS(state~p)                  f_IRS_phos_by_IR()
  R_phos_IRS_by_AA:       IRS(state~u) -> IRS(state~p)                  f_IRS_phos_by_AA()
  R_phos_IRSp_S636:       IRS(state~p) -> IRS(state~pS636)              f_IRSp_to_pS636()
  R_phos_IRSu_S636:       IRS(state~u) -> IRS(state~pS636)              f_IRSu_to_pS636()
  R_turnover_IRS_S636:    IRS(state~pS636) -> IRS(state~u)              IRS_pS636_turnover

  # --- The two PI3K activities
  R_phos_PI3K_var:        PI3K_var(s~u) -> PI3K_var(s~p)                f_PI3K_var_phos()
  R_dephos_PI3K_var:      PI3K_var(s~p) -> PI3K_var(s~u)                PI3K_variant_p_dephos
  R_phos_PI3K_PDK1:       PI3K_PDK1(s~u) -> PI3K_PDK1(s~p)              f_PI3K_PDK1_phos()
  R_dephos_PI3K_PDK1:     PI3K_PDK1(s~p) -> PI3K_PDK1(s~u)              PI3K_p_PDK1_dephos

  # --- Akt. Four phosphorylation and four dephosphorylation rules, one per
  # context; the `_first`/`_second` constants are independent (see header note).
  R_phos_Akt_T308_1:      Akt(t308~u,s473~u) -> Akt(t308~p,s473~u)      f_Akt_T308_first()
  R_phos_Akt_T308_2:      Akt(t308~u,s473~p) -> Akt(t308~p,s473~p)      f_Akt_T308_second()
  R_phos_Akt_S473_1:      Akt(s473~u,t308~u) -> Akt(s473~p,t308~u)      f_Akt_S473_first()
  R_phos_Akt_S473_2:      Akt(s473~u,t308~p) -> Akt(s473~p,t308~p)      f_Akt_S473_second()
  R_dephos_Akt_T308_1:    Akt(t308~p,s473~u) -> Akt(t308~u,s473~u)      Akt_pT308_dephos_first
  R_dephos_Akt_T308_2:    Akt(t308~p,s473~p) -> Akt(t308~u,s473~p)      Akt_pT308_dephos_second
  R_dephos_Akt_S473_1:    Akt(s473~p,t308~u) -> Akt(s473~u,t308~u)      Akt_pS473_dephos_first
  R_dephos_Akt_S473_2:    Akt(s473~p,t308~p) -> Akt(s473~u,t308~p)      Akt_pS473_dephos_second

  # --- mTORC2, with a direct amino-acid input alongside the PI3K-variant one
  R_phos_mTORC2_PI3K:     mTORC2(s2481~u) -> mTORC2(s2481~p)            f_mTORC2_by_PI3K_var()
  R_phos_mTORC2_AA:       mTORC2(s2481~u) -> mTORC2(s2481~p)            f_mTORC2_by_AA()
  R_dephos_mTORC2:        mTORC2(s2481~p) -> mTORC2(s2481~u)            mTORC2_pS2481_dephos

  # --- AMPK, with a direct amino-acid input alongside the IRS1 one. This is
  # the paper's central claim: amino acids activate AMPK, not only mTOR.
  R_phos_AMPK_IRSp:       AMPK(t172~u) -> AMPK(t172~p)                  f_AMPK_phos_by_IRSp()
  R_phos_AMPK_AA:         AMPK(t172~u) -> AMPK(t172~p)                  f_AMPK_phos_by_AA()
  R_dephos_AMPK:          AMPK(t172~p) -> AMPK(t172~u)                  AMPK_pT172_dephos

  # --- TSC1-TSC2. Two independent modifications off the same naive state,
  # each reverting directly to it.
  R_phos_TSC_S1387:       TSC(state~u) -> TSC(state~pS1387)             f_TSC_S1387()
  R_dephos_TSC_S1387:     TSC(state~pS1387) -> TSC(state~u)             TSC1_TSC2_pS1387_dephos
  R_phos_TSC_T1462:       TSC(state~u) -> TSC(state~pT1462)             f_TSC_T1462()
  R_dephos_TSC_T1462:     TSC(state~pT1462) -> TSC(state~u)             TSC1_TSC2_pT1462_dephos

  # --- mTORC1: amino acids activate, TSC1-TSC2 inactivates
  R_phos_mTORC1_AA:       mTORC1(s2448~u) -> mTORC1(s2448~p)            f_mTORC1_by_AA()
  R_dephos_mTORC1_TSC:    mTORC1(s2448~p) -> mTORC1(s2448~u)            f_mTORC1_dephos()

  # --- p70-S6K, extended module. T389 from mTORC1, T229 from PI3K/PDK1.
  R_phos_S6K_T389_1:      S6K(t389~u,t229~u) -> S6K(t389~p,t229~u)      f_S6K_T389_first()
  R_phos_S6K_T389_2:      S6K(t389~u,t229~p) -> S6K(t389~p,t229~p)      f_S6K_T389_second()
  R_phos_S6K_T229_1:      S6K(t229~u,t389~u) -> S6K(t229~p,t389~u)      f_S6K_T229_first()
  R_phos_S6K_T229_2:      S6K(t229~u,t389~p) -> S6K(t229~p,t389~p)      f_S6K_T229_second()
  R_dephos_S6K_T389_1:    S6K(t389~p,t229~u) -> S6K(t389~u,t229~u)      p70_S6K_pT389_dephos_first
  R_dephos_S6K_T389_2:    S6K(t389~p,t229~p) -> S6K(t389~u,t229~p)      p70_S6K_pT389_dephos_second
  R_dephos_S6K_T229_1:    S6K(t229~p,t389~u) -> S6K(t229~u,t389~u)      p70_S6K_pT229_dephos_first
  R_dephos_S6K_T229_2:    S6K(t229~p,t389~p) -> S6K(t229~u,t389~p)      p70_S6K_pT229_dephos_second

  # --- PRAS40. S183 from mTORC1, T246 from Akt.
  R_phos_PRAS40_S183_1:   PRAS40(s183~u,t246~u) -> PRAS40(s183~p,t246~u)  f_PRAS40_S183_first()
  R_phos_PRAS40_S183_2:   PRAS40(s183~u,t246~p) -> PRAS40(s183~p,t246~p)  f_PRAS40_S183_second()
  R_phos_PRAS40_T246_1:   PRAS40(t246~u,s183~u) -> PRAS40(t246~p,s183~u)  f_PRAS40_T246_first()
  R_phos_PRAS40_T246_2:   PRAS40(t246~u,s183~p) -> PRAS40(t246~p,s183~p)  f_PRAS40_T246_second()
  R_dephos_PRAS40_S183_1: PRAS40(s183~p,t246~u) -> PRAS40(s183~u,t246~u)  PRAS40_pS183_dephos_first
  R_dephos_PRAS40_S183_2: PRAS40(s183~p,t246~p) -> PRAS40(s183~u,t246~p)  PRAS40_pS183_dephos_second
  R_dephos_PRAS40_T246_1: PRAS40(t246~p,s183~u) -> PRAS40(t246~u,s183~u)  PRAS40_pT246_dephos_first
  R_dephos_PRAS40_T246_2: PRAS40(t246~p,s183~p) -> PRAS40(t246~u,s183~p)  PRAS40_pT246_dephos_second
end reaction rules

end model

begin actions
  generate_network({overwrite=>1})  # 33 species, 48 reactions

  # Amino-acid stimulation time course

  #@protocol: |
  #  Amino-acid readdition WITH insulin. All pools start fully
  #  dephosphorylated; both inputs are held at 1 for the whole 120 min. The
  #  insulin arm makes IR-pY1146 and Akt-pT308 transient rather than
  #  monotone, so the full window is needed to see the peak and the plateau.

  #@figure: Fig. 2b in Dalle Pezze et al. (2016)

  simulate({method=>"ode",suffix=>"ode",t_start=>0,t_end=>120,n_steps=>1200,\
    atol=>1e-10,rtol=>1e-8})
end actions
